<p>When a San Antonio home or business is damaged by hail, wind, fire, or water, the repair has to meet the building codes in force today, not the codes that applied when the building went up. That gap between "how it was built" and "how it must be rebuilt" is a frequent source of confusion on insurance claims. This page summarizes San Antonio building codes and how they interact with insurance repairs, using information published by the City of San Antonio Development Services Department (DSD) and other cited sources.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Last verified: October 20, 2026</span><p>Codes, fees, and procedures change. This page is general information, not code consulting or legal advice. Always confirm current requirements with the City of San Antonio Development Services Department, or with your own city or county if your property is outside San Antonio city limits, before starting work.</p></div>

<h2>Overview: San Antonio adopted the 2024 ICC codes</h2>
<p>On <strong>January 30, 2025</strong>, San Antonio City Council adopted the <strong>2024 International Code Council (ICC) codes</strong> and the 2024 San Antonio Property Maintenance Code (Ordinance No. 2025-01-30-0075). The City's Chapter 10 Building-Related Codes document lists an <strong>effective date of May 1, 2025</strong>. The adopted codes are amended locally, so the City's version can differ from the model ICC text. The energy code was excluded from this cycle: DSD states it remains under the <strong>2021</strong> International Energy Conservation Code.</p>

